From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 14:30:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] Report a guest access to the machine before serving it

The classic 24-bit domain is trapped by machine policy now, so every access
to it arrives here. Nobody was looking.

That is the whole of the discovery phase: a range cannot be classified as
RAM, chipset MMIO, a mirror or open bus until it is known what reaches it
and which guest code sent it. Until then, promoting a range to a direct
mapping because software touched it is guessing.

Two call sites, in the standalone pair only -- PiStorm keeps its own copies
above the #else and is not touched. The observer returns nothing and
decides nothing: Emu68 goes on to service the access exactly as it did
before, so this changes what is known and not what happens.

The filter lives on the machine side (addresses at or above 0x01000000 are
dropped there) so that neither call site carries an address constant. Emu68
learns no Amiga geography from this patch.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/src/aarch64/vectors.c
+++ b/src/aarch64/vectors.c
@@ -15,6 +15,9 @@
 #include "tlsf.h"
 #include "M68k.h"
 #include "cache.h"
+#ifdef BELLATRIX_EMU68
+#include "machine/bus.h"
+#endif
 #include "intc.h"
 
 #define FULL_CONTEXT 0
@@ -793,6 +796,10 @@
 {
     D(kprintf("[JIT:SYS] SYSWriteValToAddr(0x%x, %d, %p)\n", value, size, far));
     
+#ifdef BELLATRIX_EMU68
+    machine_bus_observe((uint32_t)far, size, 1);
+#endif
+
     if (far == 0xdeadbeef && size == 1) {
         kprintf("%c", value);
         return 1;
@@ -864,6 +871,10 @@
 int SYSReadValFromAddr(uint64_t *value, uint64_t *value2, int size, uint64_t far)
 {
     D(kprintf("[JIT:SYS] SYSReadValFromAddr(%d, %p)\n", size, far));
+
+#ifdef BELLATRIX_EMU68
+    machine_bus_observe((uint32_t)far, size, 0);
+#endif
 
     /*
         On PiStorm these two snoop the values coming back from a real Paula.
